nss_n2h: refuse the extra-pbuf sysctls before the core is up

Writing dev.nss.n2hcfg.extra_pbuf_core0 with the NSS core not brought up
takes the kernel down:

  (NULL device *): Configuring additional NSS pbufs
  Unable to handle kernel access to user memory outside uaccess routines
    at virtual address 0000000000000210
  Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000005 [#1] SMP

nss_ctx points into nss_top_main, so it is never NULL and the handler
walks straight into nss_n2h_buf_pool_cfg() with a context whose message
plumbing does not exist yet. It is easy to reach: qca-nss-pbuf writes
that sysctl from its init script at boot, and on a board where nothing
armed the data plane the core is still down when it runs (reported on
the OpenWrt forum, thread 253014, on a GL-B3000 built without
nss-tools-dwmac).

Guard both handlers the way the rest of the driver does before it uses a
context, and say so rather than dying.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>

--- a/nss_n2h.c
+++ b/nss_n2h.c
@@ -1361,6 +1361,11 @@ static int nss_n2h_buf_cfg_core0_handler
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	if (unlikely(nss_ctx->state != NSS_CORE_STATE_INITIALIZED)) {
+		nss_warning("%px: core 0 is not up, cannot add pbufs\n", nss_ctx);
+		return -EAGAIN;
+	}
+
 	if (nss_ctx->buf_sz_allocated) {
 		nss_n2h_core0_add_buf_pool_size = nss_ctx->buf_sz_allocated;
 		return -EPERM;
@@ -1400,6 +1405,11 @@ static int nss_n2h_buf_cfg_core1_handler
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	if (unlikely(nss_ctx->state != NSS_CORE_STATE_INITIALIZED)) {
+		nss_warning("%px: core 1 is not up, cannot add pbufs\n", nss_ctx);
+		return -EAGAIN;
+	}
+
 	if (nss_ctx->buf_sz_allocated) {
 		nss_n2h_core1_add_buf_pool_size = nss_ctx->buf_sz_allocated;
 		return -EPERM;
